1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,100
That email you didn't send, the calculation you misread, the realisation that the deadline was yesterday... all followed by a sinking feeling, a quickening of the breath and the realisation that it's your fault.
那封你没有发出的邮件，你误读的计算结果，意识到截止日期是昨天……随之而来的是不祥的预感，呼吸加快，意识到这是你的错。

2
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:18,480
Fear not!
别怕！

3
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:23,300
Handled well, a mistake at work can become a great opportunity.
处理得当，工作中的错误可以变成一个绝佳的机会。

4
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,560
Start by owning up to it.
首先要勇于承认错误。

5
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:35,390
It may be tempting to cover up a mistake, but any deception is unlikely to last for long and could delay attempts to put right the problem.
掩盖错误可能很有诱惑性，但任何欺骗都不太可能持续很久，而且可能会延误尝试去解决问题。

6
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:40,120
And that's next – try to resolve the problems that you've created.
接下来就是——努力解决你自己造成的问题。

7
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:46,200
Being proactive and coming to your manager with suggestions is likely to help you regain trust.
积极主动地向你的经理提出建议，很可能有助于你重新获得信任。

8
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:54,150
Psychologists tell us that we tend to have a negativity bias – we are more likely to remember negative things.
心理学家告诉我们，我们往往有消极偏见——我们更容易记住消极的事情。

9
00:00:54,150 --> 00:01:03,340
This is why executive coach Dina Denham Smith tells us we need to make our progress in fixing the issue clear and visible.
这就是为什么高管教练迪娜·德纳姆·史密斯告诉我们，我们需要让自己在解决问题上的进展清晰可见。

10
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:06,080
It's also important to apologise.
道歉也很重要。

11
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:11,600
Showing genuine remorse demonstrates that we can take ownership of our blunders.
表现出真诚的悔恨表明我们可以为自己的错误负责。

12
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:19,870
As well as looking at how we make amends for our mix-ups to other people, we also need to be honest with ourselves.
除了考虑如何弥补我们对他人的影响之外，我们也需要对自己诚实。

13
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:22,430
Removing emotion is important.
消除情绪很重要。

14
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:28,330
Shame from making a mistake can cloud our judgement when deciding how to respond.
因犯错而产生的羞耻感会在决定如何回应时影响我们的判断。

15
00:01:28,330 --> 00:01:36,520
It can also affect our perception of the mistake's seriousness – it's easy to exaggerate how bad something really is.
它还会影响我们对错误严重性的认知——人们很容易夸大事情的糟糕程度。

16
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:38,700
Everyone makes mistakes.
每个人都会犯错。

17
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:42,360
What's really important is how we address them.
真正重要的是我们如何应对错误。

18
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:44,840
It's important to be reflective.
进行反思很重要。

19
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:50,310
Consider if there's an underlying cause for the mistake, and how to prevent future slip-ups.
考虑错误是否有潜在的原因，以及如何防止将来出现失误。

20
00:01:50,310 --> 00:01:56,780
It can also be useful to seek advice from someone who's had similar experiences in the past.
向有过类似经历的人寻求建议也可能很有用。

21
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:03,990
Combined with our own reflections, this can be a powerful way to grow in our job and be more successful.
加上我们自己的反思，这可以成为在工作中成长并取得更大成功的有效途径。

22
00:02:03,990 --> 00:02:18,040
Academics Jasmine Virhia and Grace Lordan highlight research showing that workplaces which replace fear and blame around mistakes with opportunities for self-reflection are more psychologically safe.
学者贾斯敏·维尔希亚和格蕾丝·洛丹提请人们注意一项研究，该研究表明用自我反思的机会取代对错误的恐惧和责备的工作场所，会令人在心理上感到更加安全。

23
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:25,830
This allows people to learn from their mistakes, grow their skills, improve processes and ultimately productivity.
这使人们能够从错误中学习，提升技能，改进流程，最终提高生产力。

24
00:02:25,830 --> 00:02:33,900
Handled in the right way, by both employees and employers, mistakes can become a powerful tool for improvement.
如果员工和雇主都处理得当，错误可以成为进步的有效工具。
